{"title":"1965 AHSME Problems/Problem 13","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of number-pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> which satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/061d965e2d046956614079024339b2e760dfc13d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5y - 3x = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"102\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/3/d43d13dcc02a68f6dcf521719e80547f49d80ac5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + y^2 \\le 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"99\" height=\"18\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/9/0d972899b1ce7966c9be7d02699f653695d6e5bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 0 \\qquad  \\textbf{(B) }\\ 1 \\qquad  \\textbf{(C) }\\ 2 \\qquad  \\textbf{(D) }\\ \\text{more than two, but finite}\\qquad \\textbf{(E) }\\ \\text{greater than any finite number}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"764\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/f/aafe973592865590f58a9b6a59ade0f5d5a00018.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]  draw((-16,0)--(16,0), arrow=Arrows); label(&quot;$x$&quot;,(17.5,0)); draw((0,-16)--(0,16), arrow=Arrows); label(&quot;$y$&quot;, (0,17.5));  draw(circle((0,0),8),red); draw((-14,-12/5)--(14, 72/5), blue, arrow=Arrows);  [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p>For an ordered pair <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> to satisfy the restrictions, the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/3/e53b1fe25be1c679117fb44a6a886fe1247d189a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"44\" height=\"20\" /> must lie on the graph of the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/7/987cebe59cb294253a0fc54df952db1307628a4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5y-3x=15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"17\" /> (shown in blue in the diagram) and on the closed <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Disk\" title=\"Disk\">disk</a> given by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/7/d9729755e60f894a6ce77ac7169c7edc88190d0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+y^2 \\leq 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" /> (whose boundary is red in the diagram). Because the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />-intercept of the line, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/b/b3b8eb8e093d1d1c3834d880a7b448b3fa76eeab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, is within the disk (and not on the boundary of the disk), the line cannot be tangent to the disk, and so the disk must contain infinitely many of the points on the line. Thus, our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/f/c2fbb3fdfbfc456a3e6ec8c4ecf57e830b4e05e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\fbox{\\textbf{(E)} greater than any finite number}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"270\" height=\"30\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_13&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME\" title=\"1965 AHSME\">1965 AHSC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems\" title=\"1965 AHSME Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Answer_Key\" title=\"1965 AHSME Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=44&amp;year=1965\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"1965 AHSME Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AHSME_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"1965 AHSME Problems/Problem 14\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1965_AH